{  
  "distractors": [  
    {  
      "text": "They significantly improve long-term productivity by eliminating all background noise during work hours.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text explicitly states that productivity has not improved and that quiet hours only offer short-term relief, not lasting gains. Claiming they 'significantly improve long-term productivity' contradicts the author’s argument that the root issue is architectural, not auditory."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "They are widely embraced by employees as a sustainable solution to workplace distractions.",  
      "why_wrong": "The passage notes that employees report only short-term relief, and the overall stress levels remain high. The author implies skepticism toward the practice, calling it a 'symbolic concession,' which undermines the idea that it is widely embraced as a sustainable solution."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "They allow managers to avoid making costly changes to office design while still addressing employee needs.",  
      "why_wrong": "While the author criticizes quiet hours as a way to avoid redesign, the text does not claim they 'address employee needs'—in fact, it argues they fail to reduce stress and are a form of managerial evasion. This distractor misrepresents the author’s view by suggesting quiet hours are effective, even if superficial."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "They lead to a measurable decrease in unplanned interactions between coworkers, improving focus and mental clarity.",  
      "why_wrong": "The text states that employees in open offices experience 19% more unplanned interactions per day, and quiet hours do not reduce this. The author argues the problem is structural, not behavioral, so claiming quiet hours reduce unplanned interactions contradicts the evidence presented."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "They are scientifically proven to enhance memory and reading comprehension for all types of workers.",  
      "why_wrong": "While the text cites Dr. Orfield’s research on moderate noise impairing performance, it emphasizes that the real issue is unpredictability and semantic content—not just noise level. The author also argues quiet hours don’t fix the deeper problem, so claiming they 'enhance' cognitive performance for all workers is unsupported and contradicted by the text."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "They are effective because they encourage employees to take more responsibility for managing their own focus and well-being.",  
      "why_wrong": "The author criticizes quiet hours as a way of 'externalizing the cost of poor design onto employees’ time management and emotional resilience,' implying that placing the burden on individuals is a failure of leadership—not a positive outcome. This distractor misrepresents the author’s critique by framing employee responsibility as a benefit."  
    }  
  ]  
}
